## Founding & History
Charles Reuben Estate's vineyard was established in 1989. Some sources also state it was established in the late 1980s or 1990. It has been a family business producing wine for many years, with wine production dating back to the 1990s. The Volkmann family acquired the business in 2013. Following their purchase, renovations on a second dwelling on the property began immediately and it opened as tourist accommodation in 2014. The vineyard was significantly pruned to allow for reinvigoration. In April 2019, a specialized brandy distillery was added to the estate. The estate is recognized as having one of the oldest vineyards in Tasmania's Coal River Valley region.

## People
The current owners of Charles Reuben Estate are Louise and Jens Volkmann, who have managed the business for the last decade. It operates as a family-run business, with Louise and Jens, along with their three daughters, managing the daily operations. Jens Volkmann serves as the master distiller, having received his training at the University of Hohenheim in Germany. The estate also works with an acclaimed and award-winning winemaker.

## Vineyard & Site
Charles Reuben Estate is situated in the Coal River Valley, Tasmania, specifically in the Tea Tree locality. The vineyard is described as three hectares, though other sources indicate it is four hectares or ten acres. It consists of approximately 10,000 vines that were around 25 years old as of 2014, suggesting plantings around 1989. The varietals planted include Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling, and select Bordeaux varieties. Since 2015, the estate has also cultivated high-quality Lavender varieties for oil production and culinary use. The farming approach emphasizes sustainable production, minimal intervention, low food miles, passive temperature control, and solar energy, with a goal to be carbon neutral. The estate is environmentally conscious and operates on a "Paddock to Glass" philosophy.

## Cellar Door Experience
The cellar door at Charles Reuben Estate is open by appointment. It provides a personalized tasting experience in an intimate setting, allowing visitors to explore a selection of both wines and spirits. Tastings typically cost $10 and include a range of products, with 5-7 tastes recommended. Drinks by the glass are also available. Guests are advised to book at least two days in advance, or to call for earlier arrangements. Guided tours can also be arranged for those seeking a more immersive experience to learn about the winemaking and distillation processes. The cellar door and tasting room offer serene views of the vineyards and rolling hills. For those unable to visit the estate, Charles Reuben Estate also offers wine, gin, and brandy tastings every Saturday from 8:30 am to 3:00 pm at Stall 218 of Hobart's Salamanca Market.

## Food & Hospitality
While there is no permanent restaurant, Charles Reuben Estate offers food experiences during special events. For instance, during the "Spring in the Vines" festival (scheduled for October 31 - November 2, 2025), Grazing Daisy & Co's Courtney will serve a grazing menu featuring charcuterie, cheese, smoked Rannoch Quail, and chocolate, paired with the estate's wines.

The estate provides accommodation in the 'Pinot Cottage', a secluded, self-contained timber cottage located among the vines. The cottage features two bedrooms, a fully equipped European kitchen, a spa, and a wood fire. It can accommodate up to five guests and includes amenities such as a porta-cot, fire screen, stair gate, and individual electric heaters, with plans for a swing set and sand pit. Wireless internet is available in the cottage.

The estate hosts events such as the "Open Vineyards Weekend" and the "Spring in the Vines" festival, which may include live music from local groups like the Coal River Ukulele Mob (CRUM) and The Rusticators.

## Awards & Recognition
Charles Reuben Estate is an award-winning vineyard and distillery, recognized for producing award-winning premium wines and spirits. The estate works with an award-winning winemaker. Their fruit brandies (eau de vie/aqua vitae) have received several awards within their first two years of production. The Charles Reuben Estate Cottage Block Cabernet Sauvignon 2022 has been "Highly Recommended," achieving a score range of 92-94. The 'Pinot Cottage' accommodation is also described as award-winning.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Charles Reuben Estate employs sustainable production methods, including minimal intervention, low food miles, passive temperature control, and solar energy. The estate's operations are environmentally conscious, aiming for carbon neutrality. They describe their approach as "Paddock to Glass". No specific organic or biodynamic certifications were found in the provided sources.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Charles Reuben Estate is 777 Middle Tea Tree Road, Tea Tree, Tasmania, Australia, 7017.
The phone number is 0434 955 777.
Their website is charlesreuben.com.au.
General inquiries can be sent to info@charlesreuben.com.au, while accommodation inquiries can be directed to stay@charlesreuben.com.au.

The estate is located approximately 30 km northeast of Hobart, a 30-minute drive (35 km). It is about 7 minutes from Richmond, or 5 minutes northwest (8 km). Hobart Airport is a 25-30 minute drive away. Directions are available from Hobart/the airport and from Launceston/northern Tasmania. Disabled access is available; visitors should contact the operator for details.

## Notable / Distinctive
Charles Reuben Estate stands out as both an award-winning vineyard and distillery, being one of the oldest vineyards in Tasmania's Coal River Valley region. It is a family-run business that prides itself on being a small-scale producer, allowing them to focus on quality and unique styles rather than market trends. A distinctive feature is its distillery, established in 2019, which produces European-style small-batch fruit brandies and Cognac/Armagnac-style aged brandies, a result of Jens Volkmann's training as a European master distiller. The estate produces rare specialty spirits and brandies, some of which are exclusive to Charles Reuben Estate and not found elsewhere in Australia. This includes "Tisco," a Tasmanian version of Pisco. Beyond wine and spirits, the estate also functions as a lavender farm, growing high-quality lavender varieties since 2015. The 'Pinot Cottage' offers secluded, on-site accommodation, providing a unique stay amongst the vines. The estate also maintains a presence at Hobart's Salamanca Market every Saturday, offering tastings of their products.

